  "era": "mountain clan warfare era to national costume",
  "summary": "Banded cartridge pockets and flared skirts — the silhouette of every Caucasus horseman.",
  "description": "The chokha (Georgian: kartafuli chokha; Russian: cherkeska) is the fitted wool coat of the Caucasus: long nipped waist flaring over the knee, cartridge pockets (gazyr) sewn across the chest for gunpowder charges, and a row of small silver-and-black buttons that made the front a vertical ornament. Georgian versions carry sword-embroidered torso panels; Circassian ones are usually black or brown wool over a beshmet undercoat. Mountain riders wore it into every war of the 19th century, Russian Cossacks copied it, and today it is the national dress of Georgia and a badge of Caucasus identity at weddings, dances and state ceremonies.",
  "culturalContext": "The chokha is the Caucasus's shared masculine grammar — Georgian, Circassian, Chechen and Cossack variants of one horseman coat — worn in war, dance and state ceremony; it survived empire and Soviet standardization as the region's identity garment, and Georgian national dress law enshrined it for official occasions.",
